    "created_at": "2021-09-04T18:14:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-04T18:15:43Z",
    "body": "> If you change rust version you'll have to regenerate the prompt at least twice as the cache would be read first, then a background task would be spawned updating the cache of need be then that'd be displayed.\r\n\r\nI don't think we can reliably regenerate the prompt across shells and systems, which is the reason we don't have \"async prompts\" out of the box.\r\n\r\nIdeally, such a cache would be quick enough to read from before performing any work.\r\n\r\n---\r\nI had a working proof-of-concept for binary caching on the `binary-caching` branch, but the implementation turned out to be inelegant, requiring some refactoring to build the caching mechanism directly into `exec_cmd`.\r\n\r\nIf anyone would like pick this up, the caching mechanism is pretty well isolated and tested in this file on the branch:\r\nhttps://github.com/starship/starship/blob/8bb76e08eeacdbeb7b51a2aee1c7cc9dbfba2b81/crates/starship-cache/src/lib.rs\r\n\r\nAs I was doing in that branch, the cache would store command outputs and would invalidate them if the binary's metadata ever changed (filesize, created time, modified time, permissions). If the binary is the same, we could pretty confidently assume the output is the same (with the exception of context-sensitive commands like `git`). ",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
